    "evidence_text": "Amyloid-DNA Composites of Bacterial Biofilms Stimulate Autoimmunity.\n\nWe also found that the infection of lupus-prone mice with curli-producing bacteria triggered higher autoantibody titers compared to curli-deficient bacteria.\n\nEffects of Bifidobacterium animalis administered during lactation on allergic and autoimmune responses in rodents.\n\nB. animalis administration started when the mice or rats were 2 weeks old.\n\nHowever, allergen-induced cytokine production in females was not affected by B. animalis.\n\nHowever, the involved signaling pathways and effector mechanisms are still poorly understood.\n\nBasophils were also present in the lymph nodes and spleen of subjects with SLE.\n\nTLR-activated B cells suppress T cell-mediated autoimmunity.\n\nTLR sense microbial infections, and control activation of immune responses.\n\nReduction of gut commensal bacteria impaired the development of EAE.\n\nThis approach may offer a new therapeutic paradigm in the treatment of multiple sclerosis and perhaps other autoimmune conditions.\n\nInnate immunity to a facultative intracellular bacterial pathogen.\n\nThe murine response to Listeria monocytogenes has long been considered a paradigm of T-cell-mediated immunity.",
